--TEST--
`Core\Request::jsonAs<T>()` hydrates the request body into a declared type, keeps nothing of what it built, and leaves the octets to the readers after it `rule:http-server/buffering-readers-share-the-body-and-streaming-readers-consume-it`, `rule:core-classes/derive-field-list`
--HEADERS--
Content-Type: application/json
--POST_RAW--
{"name":"ada","tags":2}
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A body is a peer's bytes, so the fields that receive them declare the
// qualifier they carry: `rule:security/derived-codec-qualifiers` puts that
// question at the call site that decodes, and this is the declaration it is
// asked of.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
class Author {
    public tainted string $name;
    public int $tags;

    public function constructor(tainted string $name, int $tags) {
        $this->name = $name;
        $this->tags = $tags;
    }
}

// The member is `Core\Json::decodeAs` over the body's octets and nothing else:
// one `new` per document, with every declared field read out of it.
Author $author = Core\Request::jsonAs<Author>();
echo $author->name, " has ", $author->tags, "\n";

// It keeps nothing of what it built, which is the one row of the rule's table
// where it differs from `json()`: a second call hydrates the octets again, so
// writing to what the second answered leaves the first alone. Two callers
// handed one object would share a mutable value neither asked to share.
Author $again = Core\Request::jsonAs<Author>();
$again->name = "bob";
echo $author->name, " and ", $again->name, "\n";

// The octets are still the request's: a buffering reader may follow another,
// in either order, and each answers the same body.
echo Core\Request::body(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
ada has 2
ada and bob
{"name":"ada","tags":2}
